  "payload": {
    "rawResponseText": "For an **upper-upscale hotel project in Europe**, owners and developers commonly shortlist the major **international upper-upscale/luxury operators** plus a few strong **Europe-rooted brands** with proven regional footprint. The most frequently seen names in the results are **Accor, Marriott, Hilton, Hyatt, IHG, Four Seasons, Kempinski, Mandarin Oriental, Rosewood, and Rocco Forte**.[1][4][6][11]\n\nA practical shortlist would usually include:\n\n- **Marriott International** — especially **The Luxury Collection, W, Ritz-Carlton, St. Regis, JW Marriott, Autograph Collection**.[1][3][9]\n- **Accor** — especially **Raffles, Fairmont, Sofitel, MGallery, Emblems, Orient Express**.[1][6][7][11]\n- **Hilton** — especially **Waldorf Astoria** and **Conrad**.[1][4]\n- **Hyatt** — especially **Park Hyatt** and **Andaz**.[1][4]\n- **IHG** — especially **InterContinental** and **Kimpton**.[1][4]\n- **Four Seasons** — a recurring “top-tier” luxury choice in Europe.[2][4][10][12]\n- **Kempinski** — often considered for classic European upper-upscale/luxury positioning.[4][5]\n- **Mandarin Oriental** — common for urban luxury and resort-style projects.[4][10][12]\n- **Rosewood** — frequently shortlisted for high-end, design-led projects.[4][10]\n- **Rocco Forte Hotels** — a notable Europe-focused luxury operator.[4][5]\n\nIf you want the answer framed *specifically as an owner/developer shortlist for an upper-upscale deal* rather than a luxury-deck list, the most defensible core set is usually **Marriott, Accor, Hilton, Hyatt, and IHG**, with **Four Seasons, Kempinski, Mandarin Oriental, Rosewood, and Rocco Forte** added depending on asset quality, location, and whether the project is city, resort, or heritage-led.[1][4][6][11]",
